ally in either direction.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Illinois sits on the Democratic side of the presidential map, with a partisan lean of about six points toward Democrats. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or market prices are available for this race yet, so the forecast rests on the state's fundamental lean. The incumbent president is term-limited, making this an open contest with no incumbency advantage on either side.\n\nRecent headlines point to strong Democratic organization in the state: Governor JB Pritzker has committed $20 million to help midterm Democrats, is weighing his own 2028 run, and is credited with swinging the state's Senate race while campaigning against Trump. That activity suggests Democratic enthusiasm and resources, but it does not directly measure the presidential vote. The race remains a Democratic lean with wide uncertainty until polling arrives.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7608, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.746, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7069, "margin": 6.0}]}